Fix the UTF-8 mouse report building the row's lead byte from the column

MakeMouseReportStr() encodes the two coordinates of an extended mouse report
when DECSET 1005 (UTF-8 mouse tracking) is active. Values above 127 take a
two-byte form. The row's branch computes its lead byte from `x` instead of
`y`:

  vtterm.c:MakeMouseReportStr(), case IdMouseTrackExtUTF8

      if (y < 128) {
          tmpy[0] = y;
          tmpy[1] = 0;
      }
      else {
          tmpy[0] = ((x >> 6) & 0x1f) | 0xc0;   /* <- x, should be y */
          tmpy[1] = (y & 0x3f) | 0x80;
          tmpy[2] = 0;
      }

The column's branch four lines above is correct, so the typo is visible only
on a terminal tall enough for the row to pass 96 (the wire value is the row
plus 32). The continuation byte is still derived from `y`, so the low six bits
survive and the row is wrong by a multiple of 64 -- or, when the column is
small enough that `(x >> 6) & 0x1f` is zero, the report contains the byte 0xC0,
which is not valid UTF-8 at all and cannot be decoded by a conforming reader.

Reproduction, via the termitta oracle (which runs vtterm.c headless and can
inject mouse events). A press at window pixel (8, 1600) on a 300x200 terminal
is column 2, row 101; 101 + 32 = 133, so the row needs two bytes:

    before:  ESC [ M  <sp>  "  C0 85
    after:   ESC [ M  <sp>  "  C2 85

0xC2 0x85 decodes to U+0085 = 133. 0xC0 0x85 is an overlong form and is
rejected by any UTF-8 decoder written since 2003.

Related, and NOT changed here because it is arguably a design choice rather
than a slip: the button byte on the line below is emitted with `%c` rather
than through the same encoder, so it too becomes a lone byte above 127 once
enough modifiers are held. A host reading the report as UTF-8 -- which is what
mode 1005 asks it to do -- cannot decode that either. Fixing it would change
the wire format for existing clients, so it wants a maintainer's decision.

Tera Term 5.7.0-dev (commit 827a35b05).

--- a/teraterm/teraterm/vtterm.c
+++ b/teraterm/teraterm/vtterm.c
@@ -5588,7 +5588,7 @@ static int MakeMouseReportStr(char *buff, size_t buffsize, int mb, int x, int y)
 			tmpy[1] = 0;
 		}
 		else {
-			tmpy[0] = ((x >> 6) & 0x1f) | 0xc0;
+			tmpy[0] = ((y >> 6) & 0x1f) | 0xc0;
 			tmpy[1] = (y & 0x3f) | 0x80;
 			tmpy[2] = 0;
 		}
